  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "At the start of this episode, Chef Barinov returns to the hotel in a wheelchair after his operation in Germany. He warmly thanks the team and his future son-in-law Nikita, then rolls into the kitchen—only to meet a new head chef.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "eleonora",
      "t": "С возвращением тебя к нам, Витя!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Миша, спасибо тебе за всё.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "И вам всем большое спасибо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Но особое спасибо моему зятю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "Папа, он пока ещё не зять.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "Этот титул будет через сутки.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Давайте скорее поедем на кухню.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Хочу посмотреть на своих поваров.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "eleonora",
      "t": "Витя, подожди тут на минуту.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А где все мои ребята?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "eleonora",
      "t": "Витя, познакомься с этим человеком.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "eleonora",
      "t": "Это наш новый главный повар.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "eleonora",
      "t": "С возвращением тебя к нам, Витя!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Welcome back to us, Vitya!' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'with' before landing on 'us'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Миша, спасибо тебе за всё.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Misha, thank you for everything.' The meaning hinges on 'Misha' working together with 'everything' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "И вам всем большое спасибо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And thank you all very much.' Chef Barinov chooses the respectful plural 'you' alongside 'thank you' to keep a polite distance.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Но особое спасибо моему зятю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'But a special thank you to my son-in-law.' In Russian, the idea of 'but' flows naturally into 'son-in-law' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "Папа, он пока ещё не зять.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Dad, he is not your son-in-law yet.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'dad' and finishing on 'son-in-law'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "Этот титул будет через сутки.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'That title will be his in twenty-four hours.' Notice how Russian expresses 'this' and 'twenty-four hours' with crisp inflection rather than helper words. Chef Barinov answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Давайте скорее поедем на кухню.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Let us go to the kitchen quickly.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'let's' before landing on 'kitchen'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Хочу посмотреть на своих поваров.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I want to look at my cooks.' Chef Barinov brings out the contrast between 'want' and 'to look up' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "eleonora",
      "t": "Витя, подожди тут на минуту.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Vitya, wait here for a minute.' Eleonora anchors the line on 'wait', while 'a minute' completes the thought. Chef Barinov answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А где все мои ребята?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And where are all my guys?' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'and' before landing on 'guys'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "eleonora",
      "t": "Витя, познакомься с этим человеком.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Vitya, get acquainted with this man.' The meaning hinges on 'to get acquainted' working together with 'person' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "eleonora",
      "t": "Это наш новый главный повар.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'This is our new head chef.' In Russian, the idea of 'this' flows naturally into 'cook' without extra filler words. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
